                <pb facs="0573" n="503" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg503"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.—Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Hon. E. Dewdney to Colonel de Winton.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-05-28"><hi rendition="#small-caps">May</hi> 28,
                        1883</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Our Indians at Maple Creek are very slow to get off; they are now holding a sun
                    dance. Piepot has just promised to start in five days.</p>
                <p>They have all left the Walsh neighborhood, being afraid of the South Piegans, who
                    are out in numerous small war parties trying to steal what few horses our
                    Indians have left. Colonel Ilges, of Fort Assinniboine, has just sent word to
                    our police that another war party is on its way to Walsh. Our Indians have no
                    ammunition, and are much afraid of the Piegans. There is not much fear of our
                    Indians crossing again.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
